stimony about the  details of what he went through  we've seen books written by detainees  we've seen movies  so i wasn't surprised by the details of  it so much as by the tone of this is  what happened to me as a person  struggling with the death of my mother  struggling with what i'd done wrong with  al qaeda so his his sense of genuine  sense of remorse the vulnerability the  horror of the torture that he went  through so that is what surprised me  and professor denvale had quite an  impact on the military jury  well that's what this most remarkable  thing of all  to actually have a jury send a note to  the court  asking for in a sense clemency or at  least some sort of adjustment  recognition of his pain  is quite a staggering thing in fact i  think that will change the debate for  the future i don't think the prosecution  can be confident that when it puts on  witnesses who've been tortured the jury  will be appreciative of the  prosecution's position i mean i think  lots of evidence is now going to be  inadmissible once this story comes out  after all almost all their evidence is  derived from torture similar to homages  so what happens to those detainees if  they can't use the evidence and they  don't want to release them are they  still in a legal limbo forever  well uh the key words are forever right  um clearly they're in a legal limbo and  they always have been i think lots of  changes are happening at guantanamo i  don't think it's an accident that  lawyers are now  moving to have their clients released  because the war in afghanistan is over  and the war with al al-qaeda has been  one decade ago so i think that the  courts are going to start approving  people for release at least those not  charged by military commission and karen  greenberg what is the effort now to shut  down guantanamo uh two different  administrations have pledged to do that  it still hasn't happened what stands in  the way of that  well i i agree with mark i think the  movement towards closing guantanamo  is is starting to happen what stands in  the way is is actually the uh fact of  torture what stands in the way is the  fact that it's almost impossible to  mount these cases because the government  has claimed under uh under state secrets  that much can't be revealed and because  um evidence that is the result of  torture cannot be used in a court  proceeding and so as a result you've had  proceedings that haven't really been  able to get to the trial stage and um i  think that as torture comes into the  conversation inside the courtroom which  is what has happened here it is going to  change what's allowable and change what  happens professor dembele as you were  saying the word torture was sort of  banned by many administrations  at major news networks they couldn't use  the word a big debate about it now even  supreme court justices are just simply  saying it's torture right the guantanamo  now stands for detaining people without  charge who were tortured a few are being  charged but guantanamo they recognized  the senate select committee recognized  america and tortured at least 119 people  many of them have been through  guantanamo and been released and i think  the politics of it is changing so fast i  don't think the american people really  care  about keeping these people locked in  guantanamo and i think a president who's  ended the war in afghanistan  is probably well positioned to be able  to close guantanamo so i'm quite  optimistic for both of my clients i just  want to focus on one thing that  professor dembo said which is that you  know president  president obama got way ahead of his  skis he on his second day of his  presidency i'm going to close guantanamo  and we then provide  the process whereby guantanamo will be  closed  no later than one year from now  biden isn't doing that he's going about  this in a very kind of quieter  procedural way  and um so we don't actually know what  arrangements are being made or what will  